The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ret = amdgpu_vm_lock_pd(peer_vm, &exec, 2);
ret = amdgpu_amdkfd_bo_validate(bo, domain, false);
ret = process_validate_vms(process_info, &exec.ticket);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs when page table buffer objects (BOs) are evicted but not validated before updating the page tables. The first statement attempts to lock the page directory (PD) of the virtual memory (VM), which can fail if the VM is in an evicting state. The second statement validates the BOs, but if they are not validated before the update, it leads to the `amdgpu_vm_update_range` function returning -EBUSY. The last statement validates the VMs, which can also fail if the BOs are not ready, causing the `restore_process_worker` to enter a dead loop.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
/* Validate BOs managed by KFD */
list_for_each_entry(mem, &process_info->kfd_bo_list, validate_list) {
    ret = amdgpu_amdkfd_bo_validate(bo, domain, false);
}
ret = process_validate_vms(process_info, &exec.ticket);
```
Reason:
The fix separates the validation of BOs and the update of page tables into distinct steps. The first code block ensures that all BOs are validated before any updates are attempted, preventing the VM from being in an evicting state during the update. The second statement validates the VMs after the BOs have been confirmed as ready, ensuring that the `amdgpu_vm_update_range` function does not encounter an -EBUSY error, thus avoiding the dead loop in `restore_process_worker`.

